Point                                                 	Detail
Who can create it	                                     A System Administrator access level with a Standard plan or higher
Where	                                                 Setup > Processes > Milestone Paths
Active                                                 setting On by default. Inactive paths can’t be attached to projects.
Groups	                                               They control which groups can see and use the path
Changes are system-wide                               Changing a path affects the entire Workfront system, so be careful editing one that is in use
Keep stages general                                  	One path serves many projects, so use general stages like “Design Approved”
Project status                                       	The project must be in Planning or Current to have a path attached
One project, one path                                	A project’s settings show a single Milestone Path field
One milestone, one task                              	You can’t attach the same milestone to several tasks
Order                                                	Milestones can be reordered by dragging
Color                                               	Colors help visuals and reports. If you don’t pick one, the system reuses the last color used in a path.




                                      Task assignments
assigning a particular task to a user, team, or job role so that someone is responsible for completing that work.
Best practice instead of assigne
Assignment: The user, team, or role associated with completing the task.
Task Owner: The person responsible for managing or coordinating the task.

Primary assignee :
A task can have several assignees, but one of them is the primary assignee.
Primary assignee	Only a user or a job role,
A team cannot be the primary assignee


When a user, job role or team is deactivated after assignment, they stay on the work item. They are not removed. Adobe recommends reassigning the work to active resources.


                                      Work Schedules, Holidays & Time Zones
Work shedule

A work schedule defines which days and hours count as working time, and which days are non-working days such as weekends and holidays.

Work schedule = the calendar of working time.or example, imagine your organization follows this schedule:
Day	Working hours
Monday	9 AM – 6 PM
Tuesday	9 AM – 6 PM
Wednesday	9 AM – 6 PM
Thursday	9 AM – 6 PM
Friday	9 AM – 6 PM
Saturday	Non-working day
Sunday	Non-working day


Types of Work Schedules

A. Default Schedule :An organization can configure a standard schedule for its workforce.Example: Monday to Friday, 9 AM to 6 PM.
B. User Schedule : An individual may have a different working schedule. Example: One employee works Monday to Friday, while another works Sunday to Thursday.
C. Project Schedule: A project can use a schedule appropriate for the project team and its working arrangements.

Holidays?
Holidays are non-working days configured in a schedule.

Example: Suppose your task starts on Thursday and takes two working days. Friday is a company holiday.
The calculation would be:
Day	Task progress
Thursday	Day 1
Friday	Holiday – no work
Saturday	Weekend – no work

Time Zones?
A time zone represents the local time used by a person or organization.
Imagine:
- Project manager: India (IST)
- Designer: Australia (AEST)   The same scheduled meeting can appear at different local times for each person.



                                      Baseline
A Baseline is a snapshot of a project's plan at a particular point in time.
Create the initial baseline after the project plan

Why do we use Baselines?
Baselines help us:
1. Compare original planned dates with current dates.
2. Identify schedule delays.
3. Track changes in project scope and schedule.
4. Evaluate project performance.
5. Support project reporting.

What information does a Baseline capture?
- Planned start and completion dates ,Task duration,Planned hours ,Other project and task details used for comparison.



                                     Gantt and calendar views

Gantt Chart 
          Gantt Chart is a visual representation of project tasks, their start dates, completion dates, durations and dependencies.

Why do we use Gantt Charts?
1. Visualize the complete project schedule.
2. Understand task dependencies.
3. Identify overlapping tasks.
4. Monitor project timelines.
5. Identify scheduling conflicts and possible delays.

Important elements of a Gantt Chart

Task bar :Shows the planned duration of a task.
Dependency line: Shows the relationship between dependent tasks.
Milestone :Marks an important project event or achievement.
Timeline:Shows the calendar dates along which tasks are scheduled.

Complete list of options

1	Assignments	Users assigned to tasks
2	Baseline	Original saved project schedule (if we have multiple baseline only one can be select)
3	Commit Date	Date by which the assignee commits to completing a task
4	% Complete	Task completion percentage
5	Critical Path	Tasks that determine the project completion date
6	Milestone Diamonds	Diamond symbols representing milestones
7	Milestone Lines	Lines indicating milestone dates on the timeline
8	Predecessors	Dependencies between tasks
9	Progress Status	Visual indication of task progress status
10	Planned Dates	Planned task start and completion dates



Important Gantt Chart colours

Grey – Baseline Represents the saved original project schedule.
Red – Critical Path Highlights tasks that can affect the project's overall completion date.
Light Blue – Non-critical tasks  In the Project List Gantt Chart, tasks outside the critical path appear as light-blue lines.
Green – On Time  Indicates a task is progressing on schedule.
Yellow – Behind Indicates the task is falling behind its expected progress.
Light Blue – At Risk Indicates a potential scheduling problem.
Red – Late Indicates the task is late.

Other visual indicators

Green triangle	Actual start or completion date
Diamond	Milestone Connecting line	Task predecessor relationship
Marker	Assignee's Commit Date
